The timer puts its own core's nearest deadline right and leaves the other cores' as they were,
so a core whose line another core's timer fired wakes for a deadline already past;
each core's nearest deadline lies ahead of the count.

Caught-by: fuzz-smp2
Report: the timer wakes for a deadline already past
Witness: fuzz-smp2 cores-timer-across

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -980,7 +980,7 @@ void sched_time(uint64_t now)
     core->wake_stale = true;
     for (uint32_t c = 0; c < CORES; c++) {
         LOOP_BOUND(CORES);
-        cores[c].nearest_deadline = COUNT_NEVER;
+        core->nearest_deadline = COUNT_NEVER;
     }
     for (uint32_t i = 0; i < TIMER_LINES; i++) {
         LOOP_BOUND(TIMER_LINES);
